/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (1. CMOS vs CCD Sensors)
/Pg 8 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
96 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Image formation begins at the hardware level with sensors. CCD \(Charge-Coupled Device\) sensors have traditionally been used in applications requiring high-quality images and low noise. hey transfer charge across the sensor array to a single output node, producing cleaner signals but consuming more power. TOn the other hand, CMOS \(Complementary Metal-Oxide-Semiconductor\) sensors use multiple amplifiers at each pixel, resulting in lower cost, reduced power consumption, and higher speed. This makes CMOS sensors the preferred choice in most modern cameras and mobile devices, even though CCDs are still used in scientific and medical imaging.)
/Pg 8 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
97 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (2. Sampling and Quantization)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
98 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (When an image is captured, it exists in a continuous form. To process it digitally, two steps are required: sampling and quantization.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
99 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 291 0 R
/E (Sampling divides the image into a discrete grid of pixels, determining spatial resolution. A higher sampling rate captures more detail, while a lower rate may cause aliasing.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
100 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 292 0 R
/E (Quantization assigns intensity or color values to each sampled pixel. The number of quantization levels \(e.g., 8-bit = 256 levels\) directly affects image quality. Poor quantization leads to banding and loss of detail. Together, sampling and quantization convert a real-world scene into a digital format suitable for computer processing.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
